>> title: Tom Yum Goong
>> category: Soups
>> tags: shrimp, thai, sour, chiles, stovetop
>> counters: Thai Kitchen
>> aka: tom yum, tom yam, tom yum kung, tom yam goong, hot and sour shrimp soup, tom yum soup, tom yum nam sai
>> pairs-with: tom-kha-gai
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> step.1: fry the shells 3 min
>> step.2: simmer 15 min, then strain
>> step.3: steep 10 min
>> step.4: poach 3 min
>> step.5: season off the heat

Fry @shrimp heads and shells{1%cup}(from 1 lb shell-on shrimp) in @vegetable oil{1%Tbs} in a #saucepan{} over medium heat for ~fry{3%min}, pressing them until they turn red and the oil goes orange. This is where the flavour of the dish comes from, and it is thrown away in most versions of it.

Simmer @&(~1)shells{} in @water{6%cups}(1.4 L) for ~simmer{15%min}, then strain the stock and put it back in the pan.

Steep @lemongrass{3%stalks}(bruised and cut into lengths), @galangal{8%slices}(fresh or frozen; not ginger) and @makrut lime leaves{6}(torn) in @&(~1)stock{} for ~steep{10%min}. The aromatics are the same three as tom kha gai; the difference is that nothing creamy follows them.

Poach @shrimp{1%lb}(450 g; peeled) and @oyster mushrooms{4%oz}(115 g; torn) in @&(~1)broth{} for ~poach{3%min}, until the shrimp curl and go opaque.

Season @&(~1)soup{} off the heat with @fish sauce{3%Tbs}, @lime juice{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @Thai chiles{5}(crushed) and @cilantro{1/4%cup}. This is the clear one — nam sai. Lime goes in off the heat every time, because a boiled lime turns bitter.
